.Dd September 22, 2026
.Dt DSCLI 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dscli
.Nd manage NextBSD DirectoryServices accounts, servers and clients
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Cm init
.Op Fl -admin-user Ar name
.Op Fl -real-name Ar string
.Op Fl -password Ar string | Fl -no-password
.Nm
.Cm status | list
.Nm
.Cm user Cm list
.Nm
.Cm user Cm show Ar name
.Nm
.Cm user Cm add Ar name
.Op Fl -admin
.Op Fl -uid Ar n
.Op Fl -gid Ar n
.Op Fl -shell Ar path
.Op Fl -real-name Ar string
.Op Fl -password Ar string | Fl -no-password
.Nm
.Cm user Cm delete Ar name Op Fl -remove-home
.Nm
.Cm user Cm passwd Ar name Op Fl -no-password
.Nm
.Cm user Cm verify Ar name
.Nm
.Cm user Cm edit Ar name
.Op Fl -real-name Ar string
.Op Fl -shell Ar path
.Op Fl -uid Ar n
.Op Fl -gid Ar n
.Nm
.Cm group Cm list
.Nm
.Cm group Cm show Ar name
.Nm
.Cm group Cm add Ar name Op Fl -gid Ar n
.Nm
.Cm group Cm delete Ar name
.Nm
.Cm group Cm addmember Ar group Ar user
.Nm
.Cm group Cm removemember Ar group Ar user
.Nm
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Cm promote Op Fl -name Ar displayname
.Nm
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Cm demote
.Nm
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Cm join Op Ar host Op Fl -yes
.Nm
.Op Fl -dry-run
.Cm leave
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
manages the accounts that NextBSD keeps in its DirectoryServices plists,
and turns a machine into a directory server, or a client of one.
.Pp
Users and groups live in
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist
and
.Pa Groups.plist .
They are read by
.Xr nss_directory_services 5
through
.Xr nsswitch.conf 5 ,
so
.Xr id 1 ,
.Xr getent 1 ,
.Xr login 1
and
.Xr sudo 8
see them like any other account.
Root and the system users stay in
.Xr master.passwd 5 .
On a machine joined to a directory server, the same files under
.Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices
are used instead, mounted read-only from the server, and accounts are
edited on the server.
.Pp
Every command that writes must be run as root.
With
.Fl -dry-run ,
the network commands print what they would do and change nothing.
.Ss Local accounts
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm init
Create
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices
and
.Pa /Local/Users ,
the two plists, the
.Li admin
group (gid 5000) and a first administrator who is a member of it.
The name, real name and password are prompted for on a terminal;
scripts pass
.Fl -admin-user ,
.Fl -real-name
and
.Fl -password
or
.Fl -no-password .
An empty password lets the user log in without one.
.It Cm user Cm add Ar name
Create a user.
The default shell is
.Pa /bin/zsh ;
the uid is the first free one from 1001, checked against both plists and
every other name service; a private group of the same name and gid is
created; the home
.Pa /Local/Users/ Ns Ar name
is created with the skeleton dot files from
.Pa /usr/share/skel .
.Fl -admin
adds the user to
.Li admin ,
which also grants
.Li wheel
and
.Xr sudo 8 .
The password is prompted for unless
.Fl -password
or
.Fl -no-password
is given; when standard input is not a terminal it is read from there.
.It Cm user Cm delete Ar name
Remove the user from the plist and from every group; the private group
goes with it when nothing else uses it.
The home is kept unless
.Fl -remove-home
is given.
The last member of
.Li admin
cannot be deleted.
.It Cm user Cm passwd Ar name
Set the password (SHA-512 crypt, as Gershwin's dscli writes it), or with
.Fl -no-password
remove it so the user logs in without one.
.It Cm user Cm verify Ar name
Read a password from the terminal or standard input and exit 0 when it
matches; for scripts and for Gershwin's SudoAskPass.
.It Cm user Cm edit Ar name
Change the real name, shell, uid or gid.
.It Cm group Cm add , Cm delete , Cm addmember , Cm removemember
Manage groups and their members.
The
.Li admin
group and any user's primary group cannot be deleted.
.It Cm status
Report whether this machine is standalone, a directory server, or joined
to one, and which directory is in use.
.El
.Ss Directory server
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm promote Op Fl -name Ar displayname
Create
.Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices ,
seeded from the local plists; write
.Pa /etc/exports
so that directory (read-only) and
.Pa /Local/Users
are served over NFS; enable the
.Li org.nextbsd.rpcbind ,
.Li org.nextbsd.mountd
and
.Li org.nextbsd.nfsd
jobs with
.Nm launchctl Cm load Fl w ,
which persists across reboots; and announce the server over Bonjour as
.Li _nextbsd-ds._tcp
by writing a service file under
.Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/mDNSResponder/Services
for
.Xr mDNSResponder 8
to hold.
Nothing is changed when any of the three jobs is not installed.
.It Cm demote
Withdraw the announcement, disable the three jobs and remove
.Pa /etc/exports .
.Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices
is kept.
.El
.Ss Client
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Cm join Op Ar host
Without
.Ar host ,
browse for servers over Bonjour and let the user pick one
.Pq Fl -yes No takes a single result without asking .
Record the server in
.Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Binding.plist ,
enable
.Li org.nextbsd.network-mount
so the mounts return after a reboot, mount the server's directory on
.Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices
and its
.Pa /Local/Users
on
.Pa /Network/Users
now, and make the server the preferred time source in
.Xr ntp.conf 5 .
.It Cm leave
Disable the mount job, unmount both, remove the binding and restore the
default time sources.
Accounts come from
.Pa /Local
again at once.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/Local/Library/Preferences/mDNSResponder/Services/" -compact
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Users.plist
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Groups.plist
The local accounts.
Hashes are world-readable, as in Gershwin.
.It Pa /Local/Library/DirectoryServices/Binding.plist
The server this client is joined to.
.It Pa /Network/Library/DirectoryServices/
The directory served to clients, or mounted from the server.
.It Pa /Local/Users/ , Pa /Network/Users/
Home directories.
.It Pa /etc/exports
Written by
.Cm promote .
.It Pa /Local/Library/Preferences/mDNSResponder/Services/org.nextbsd.directory.plist
The Bonjour announcement.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on failure, 2 on a usage error.
.Cm user Cm verify
exits 1 when the password does not match.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Set up a machine with an administrator, then add a user who can
.Xr sudo 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# dscli init
# dscli user add jane --admin
.Ed
.Pp
Make it a directory server, and join it from another machine:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
server# dscli promote --name Office
client# dscli join
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getent 1 ,
.Xr id 1 ,
.Xr launchctl 1 ,
.Xr crypt 3 ,
.Xr exports 5 ,
.Xr nsswitch.conf 5 ,
.Xr mDNSResponder 8 ,
.Xr mountd 8 ,
.Xr nfsd 8 ,
.Xr rpcbind 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
was written for Gershwin by Joe Maloney and ported to NextBSD as plain C,
without GNUstep or gdomap.
